<greeny@top.cis.syr.edu> writes:
> 
> gmitchel@garnet.acns.fsu.edu writes:
> 
> Your characterization of limited public forums is simply incorrect.  Limited
> public forums *are* specifically designated for the purposes of expression. 
> You seem to be confusing limited public forums with nonpublic forums.
> 
> From San Diego Committee v.  Governing Bd. (citations omitted):
> 
> "A limited public forum may, depending on its nature and the nature of the
> state's actions, be open to the general public for the discussion of all
> topics, or there may be limitations on the groups allowed to use the forums
> or the topics that can be discussed.  Thus, a limited public forum may be
> open to certain groups for the discussion if any topic, or to the entire
> public for the discussion of certain topics, or some combination of the two."
> 
Specifically designated does not mean necessarily that is their primary
purpose.  It may or may not.  Conference rooms in public libraries are 
often specifically designated as public forums.

The statement from San Diego Committee v. Governing Board is very revealing.

(1) I do not believe this case has been sustained on appeal to the U.S.
Supreme Court.

(2) The limitations on the groups, I believe, refers to groups using the
same facility at the same time.  Thus, a library can allow its conference
rooms to be reserved.  When those rooms are reserved by one group, other
groups can be excluded.  This does have its parallel on Usenet.  It is
called the moderated newsgroup.

It is very dangerous to take one paragraph out of an opinion and offer it
as evidence.


Mitch

