> If by police, you mean play kindergarten hall monitor re. newsgroup
> charters, I would say no.  Their best option is to stay out of it altogether.
> Once they become Usenet mediators, they get sucked into a black hole for
> their attention.
> 
> Let me illustrate.  Site X receives several complaints from a handful of
> incensed people in Usenet newsgroup Y.  If the system administrator responds
> in any way other than to tell this handful of incensed people to take their
> fight elsewhere, the likely result is further demands for system administrator
> action.  The system administrator can admonish the offending user.  The user
> can ignore the advice or even challenge the system administrator.  Assume
> she ignores the system administrator.  More complaints come in.  Now the
> system administrator must tell these people to go away or must deal further
> with the offending user.  So the system adminstrator acts.  Now a decision
> must be made.  Once the decision is made, the user is likely to contest
> this decision.  Now the system administrator has to deal with others in
> the organization, both to defend his actions and to decide on a further
> course of action.  Copies of the offending messages must be pulled off 
> Usenet.  The group charter must be pulled via FTP.  Memos must be 
> written.  Assume the user loses the appeal.  If the user wants to
> put the system administrator's decision to the test, the user can sue.
> Since most system administrators do not own the systems they administer,
> the owners now become actively involved.  Lawyers become involved.  The
> likely result for the system administrator is not a note from the owners
> saying, "Well done!  Stand up for netiquette, no matter how much the cost."
> The more likely result is a stormy meeting with the owners wondering what
> the hell the system administrator had in mind interfering in a petty,
> juvenile squabble in the first place.  Then, assume the administrator
> prevails though it all.  The intolerant subset of users now sees that their
