> We are now entering the twilight zone, a hazy area
> inhabited by that ancient monster Censorship, where
> morality, taste, politics, and freedom of speech battle
> it out.  Erotic and political art are the usual targets
> of the censor.  Hitler closed art schools and persecuted
> artists, dictators in Latin America habitually make
> dissenting political artists "disappear", and the US art
> scene has always been rife with controversies surrounding
> obscenity in art.
> 
> Here in Montreal, we tend to be more offended by politics
> than sex.  During the Attila Lukacs show (earlier this
> year at Musee d'art contemporain), for example, the
> swastikas were the cause of controversy, rather than
> the homo-erotic nature of his works.  This does not,
> however, mean that Montreal artists are not affected
> by the less-than-liberal standards that exist outside
> Quebec's borders.
> 
> Local artists Yves Tessier and Martha Moore were recently
> caught in the crossfire when their works were censored
> by a group of Toronto artists with whom they were
> participating in an art exchange.  Another case of fear
> of censorship affected the _Drawing_and_Whoring_ show
> at Stornaway Gallery.  New York based artist Craig feared
> that if he shipped his homo-erotic work to Montreal it
> would be seized by customs officials; if he transported
> it with him and was refused entry, at least he could keep
> his work.  Unfortunately, he fell ill and the work never
> made it to Montreal.
> 
> What caused this frenzy of self-censorship and paranoia
> in Toronto was the arrest of artist Eli Langer.  Artists
> are censoring each other and themselves so as not to
> be labelled degenerate, publicity-seeking perverts and,
> conversely, mediocre artists often use controversial
> imagery to gain unwarranted media attention and to further
> their shabby little careers.  The motivation and integrity 
> of the Toronto artists must be called into question if
> you consider that both Tessier's (_Les_somnambules_) and
> Moore's (_Maman_) offending works were present at the May
> opening of _La_Proie_ in Toronto, turned to face the wall.
> Consequently, the viewing of the works became a peepshow,
> creating a context of illicit allure that neither artist
> ever intended, which could have possibly damaged the work.
> Refusing to exhibit a work is their prerogative, destroying
> it is not.  Secondly, the Toronto artists failed to look
> at the work in context.
> 
> Tessier's painting of a young boy with his head in a woman's
> crotch is done by a man.  Tessier contends that it is a
> depiction of a childhood fantasy of his; the object of lust
> is the woman.  Is a child's sexual fantasy pedophilia?
> What strikes me is that there is, in North America, a general
> paranoia and denial of children's sexuality.  Recognizing a
> child's sexuality does not make you a pedophile, abusing it does.
> 
> In North America, the noisiest controversy surrounds
> art that is homo-erotic or work perceived as pedophilia.
> The most prominent example in the US was photographer
> Robert Mapplethorpe's 1990 retrospective at Cincinnati's
> Contemporary Arts Center.  The Center and its director were
> charged with pandering obscenity and showing minors in a
> state of nudity.  They were acquitted by a jury.  The
> central issue of that trial was the question, "What is art?"
