gmitchel@garnet.acns.fsu.edu wrote:
><coasty@cyberspace.com> writes:
[...]
>So, they posted a message in X.  They set the follow-up line in that message
>to Y and Z, so any replies would go to Y and Z instead of X.  I have no
>problem with them deciding to post in X.  I have a problem with them
>attempting to control where someone else's reply goes.

They aren't "attempting to control", they are attempting to INFLUENCE.
There is a big difference.  They don't have the authority to CONTROL
you or anyone else.  Without the ability to control, censorship is not
an issue.

[...]
>                              They set the follow-up line to groups where
>their message was not sent.  They tried to determine not where their message
>went (which we agree is 100% legitimate) but where my reply went (somewhere
>they knew I would not want it to go, it is important to note).

They tried to INFLUENCE where followups would be directed, which is
exactly the purpose of the Followup-To: line.  You have COMPLETE control
over where your message is posted, they have NONE.  If you choose not to
accept their suggestion, their attempt at influencing you has failed.
If you choose to accept their suggestion--even if you do so implicitly
by not exercising your option of checking and modifying the Followup-To:
line--they've succeeded.  Whatever the case, it is your choice which
determines whether or not they succeeded in their attempt.

-Steve Coile

