[ Big, fat, juicy disclaimer ]
The following is simply my interpretation of both legal precedent
and past Usenet discussions.  I am not a lawyer.  Carl and Mitch
have posted pointers to various references concerning the limited 
public forum doctrine; you should read those before forming your 
own opinion.  In short, this is my opinion; apply grains of salt 
to taste.  8) 
[ End disclaimer ]

Most unmoderated newsgroups, at most sites, have no _a priori_ 
administration; in other words, there is no pre-publication editorial 
control.  So there is no daily, routine "administration" per se.

However, the limited public forum doctrine delegates to the forum 
maintainers the authority to maintain topicality within each forum 
they provide.

How does this apply to Usenet?  I'm glad you asked.  8)

I'm no lawyer, but I believe that the limited public forum doctrine
fits Usenet rather well.  Since Usenet has no central set of "forum
maintainers," who fills that role?  I believe that, at this time,
each site's news administrator is the "forum maintainer" for that 
site.  *IF* that forum maintainer chose to enforce topicality, I 
would suggest that only the contributions from his site are subject 
to his authority; messages from other sites have already (presumably) 
met the conditions set by those sites' forum maintainers.

For unmoderated newsgroups at most Usenet sites, the forum maintainers
do not exercise these rights in advance; that is, they do not actively
screen messages in transit.  This is well and good; the vast majority
of participants respect the topicality of the forums.  However, they
do not abdicate their rights under this scheme; they may still take
action, once a problem is identified.  This is in keeping with the
laws/regulations of the "real world;" few things are under constant
scrutiny by the relevant authorities, but they will resolve any prob-
lems as they occur.

I realize that this does not provide the most level playing field;
vnet.net may have more (or less) rigid standards of topicality than
does engr.uky.edu.  This may also vary from group to group; I may
take rec.railroad far more seriously than you, but you may be quite
vigilant about alt.censorship.  However, this view reduces the problem 
to one of sites, rather than one of each individual user.  

It should also be noted that, in the best of all possible worlds,
the news/system administrator would NOT be the person making these
decisions.  Ideally, Usenet (and other online forums) would take
their place as something of a student publication, with the same
checks and balances found in that domain.
 
To me, applying this doctrine to Usenet provides the best compromise
among the rights, responsibilities and concerns of both the users and
the service providers.

--Wes

