Wrong.  In Perry, the Supreme Court ruled that the school mail faciltities
were a nonpublic forum and found that the distinction between an elected
collective bargaining representative and rival group was sufficient to refuse
access to the latter.  The Court reversed the circuit court's ruling, and
PLEA lost its bid for access to the school mail facilities.

As for San Diego Committee, the court found that the newspaper in question
constituted a limited public forum.  Since the administration had allowed
military advertisements, the court found that the administration had opened
up the forum for discussion of the topic of the military.  It was, therefore,
a clear instance of viewpoint discrimination, to disallow anti-military
material.

The San Diego decision clearly supports the position that a limited public
forum can be restricted by topic.


For those reading this thread, I trust that this will adequately demonstrate
the extent and accuracy of Mitch's knowledge of the law related to these
matters.

 
