tradition that the Court was describing when it talked about streets and
parks which have "immemorially been held in trust for the use of the public,
and, time out of mind, have been used for the purposes of assembly,
communicating thoughts of public citizens, and discussing public questions."
Nor has there been any government fiat establishing such.

Rather, such facilities were just opened for such use, consistent with the
Court's characterization of limited public forums as "public property which
the state has opened for use by the public as a place for expressive
activity."

Do you seriously accuse me of quoting out of context, only to reply
*yourself* with this argument which conveniently omits contradictory
excerpts?

 
