Look at it this way:

	If one crossposts an article among 25 newsgroups, anyone
	reading any of those 25 newsgroups - with a decent news-
	reader - will see it but once.  

This is all well and good; the author's words are delivered to the 
recipient.  Only one copy of the message needs to be stored in the
host system's news spool; this leaves more space for the speech of
others.

	Posting 25 copies of the messages, on the other hand, will
	present it to the reader up to 25 times; this, of course,
	depends on the number of posted-to newsgroups read by the
	recipient.  

This technique, on the other hand, repeatedly places the author's
words in front of the reader - up to 25 times, if the reader actu-
ally peruses every group to which the article was posted.  In ad-
dition, each host along the way will have to retain 25 copies of
the messages (assuming that they distribute all 25 groups); this
side effect may, if overused/abused, prevent other postings from
enjoying the lifetime they would normally be expected to hold.  It
seems, therefore, that this could be an interruption to the speech
of others.

One's right to speak - even to speak loudly - should not be used to
prevent/hinder the speech of others, eh?  After all, do they not en-
joy the same rights as you?

I think that Carl's position is consistent with his earlier comments.

Here's a thought to chew on...what if I wrote a program that:
	- went through my spool, looking for messages of identical size;
	- upon finding exact matches, constructed and inserted Newsgroups
	  lines that enforced crossposting?
Anyone reading any of the groups to which you posted *would* see your
message, but they'd only see it once.

Posting a message to Usenet is, essentially, asking other computer
systems to provide it to their users.  This is all well and good,
but I see no problem with a system that says, "I'll be happy to
provide it to them, but I'm not going to do it XXX times."

