No.  Not at all.  What were the specifics of Perry?  This is crucial, if we
are to separate dicta from holdings.  This is essential if we are to
ascertain the specific circumstances under which Perry applies.

The question in Perry was: Does a university policy that denies use of
university facilities by teacher unions but permits use of those
facilities by other teacher organizations violate the free-speech
clause of the First Amendment?  The unequivocal answer of the court was?
Yes!!!!!!!!!!!!!

Now, to the reasoning.  The District Court held that the university policy
violated not only the free-speech clause of the First Amendment, it also
violated the Equal Protection clause of the Fourteenth Amendment (a point
that seems to have escaped this debate so far on spamming).  The Supremes
agreed.  So why all of this distinction between public forums, limited
public forums, and nonpublic forums?  To deny the argument that university
classrooms were nonpublic forums or, if they were limited forums, that the
university could exercise control over who was granted access.  The Supremes
asserted, that if universities do not permit other teacher organizations to
use their facilities, then and only because university facilities themselves
are not considered traditional public forums, universities could 
constitutionally deny use of such facilities to a teacher' union.  However,
if universities open up their facilities to any teacher's group to
such a degree that they can be considered public forums, then the
univeristies have to open them up on the same terms to any teacher's group
that requests use of the facilities, precisely because the First Amendment 
prohibits government officials from favoring one speaker over another.

The reasoning in San Diego Committee is even more telling.  San Diego
Committee involved a student newspaper that presented only one side of
a controversial issue.  The student newspaper refused to allow opponents
to place an advertisement expressing their viewpoint.  The language that
has been quoted here on limited public forums, "A limited public forum may, 
depending on its nature and the nature of the state's actions, be open to
the general public for the discussion of all topics, or there may be
limitations on the groups allowed to use the forums or the topics that can
be discussed. Thus, a limited public forum may be open to certain groups
for the discussion of any topic, or to the entire public for the
discussion of certain topics, or some combination of the two.  This language
refers not to something like Usenet.  It refers to something like a student
newspaper at a state university.  A student newspaper can restrict its
reporting to student affairs.  It can restrict to accept articles from
non-students, etc.  Why?  Because the student newspaper is established to
serve the needs of university students and not the needs or interests of
the four corners of the globe.  The appellate court ruled that the newspaper
acted improperly.  Why?  Because it held itself out to be an open forum for
a certain class of individuals (i.e., students, faculty, and staff) and then
proceeded to restrict the viewpoints of a subset of those individuals.

It is interesting to note, that both of the cases that you and Carl and
others cite on this subject actually lifted attempts to impose restrictions
on free speech.  They were not used by the Supreme Court or the Court of
Appeals to impose restrictions on free speech in limited public forums.


> It would seem apparent that your characterization of Usenet is inconsistent
