I told you to go read the US Code for the same reason I suggest you read
court decisions in full rather than snippets via EFF's FTP server.  I suggest
in both cases that you read annotated versions, since they will point you
to other cases that bear on relevant points in the decisions.

My perception of what you and Carl and others are doing is rambling through
decisions and pulling out paragraphs that support whatever position you claim.
That is not the proper way to conduct legal research.  It is important to
identify the parties, identify the relevant fact, obtain the holding, and
THEN proceed to the legal theory.  When reading the legal theory, you must
keep the distinction between dictum and holding in mind.  The holding is
precedent, dictum is not.  Also, keep in mind that the holding is not so
easily separated from the facts.  I have perused the holdings of EFF's
FTP server.  While they are a valuable net resource, they miss very
important components (key facts, citations, etc.).

The discussion above points to only one element of my legal theory on
First Amendment and Usenet access at universities.  It does not address
the topics of civil rights statutes or the Equal Protection clause of the
US Constitution.  I only have so much time.  It is lack of available time
and not fear that prevents me from going into those elements with as much
detail as I would like.


