In a sense, we are.  If memory serves (since I will no longer rely upon 
EFF's summaries to refresh my memory) Perry involved questions of access
to several different kinds of facilities.  One was rooms and another was
school mail.  The Supremes rightly argued that both were limited
public forums -- the important distinction not being as Carl alleges
length of time the forum is in existence but whether one of the primary
purposes of the facility was public assembly and debate.  Tradition is
also important, as some facilities such as the docks at sea ports have
served as primary places for assembly and debate in the past.  In the
case of rooms, the Supremes argued that if the university opens them to
one teacher's group, it must open them to all.  In other words, if it
claims to be a public forum or acts like a public forum, it then becomes 
a public forum.


> "Conversely on government property that has not bee made a public forum, not
