The crucial language here, I believe, is ". . . which relate to the special
purpose for which the property is used."  The language ". . . the difference
in status between the exclusive bargaining representative and its rival is
such a distinction," is also important.

And now we get to the rub . . .

Campus mail facilities at universities are not intended as a primary function
to be used as a public forum.  They facilitate the transfer of memos, messages,
etc. between faculty, administrators, and students.  Their use is not intended
for those outside the university.  The exclusive bargaining representative
gains access to mail, offices, phones, etc. as part of its collective
bargaining agreement with the university.  The university is well within its
rights to exclude outside groups from using its campus mail facilities, IMO
and in the opinion of the Supremes, unless the university chooses to open
those facilities to the public.  Once they do that, their ability to curtail
access is severely restricted.

