Earlier this week, I started a mailing list running off my workstation
called 'midwest-queers', which is a somewhat information discussion forum
for lesbian, gay, bisexual, and transgender peoples living in the midwest. 
It's designed to provide a forum for networking and basically whatever
babble might come up (I'm including the introductory information below.)

Now, in this week, it's had perhaps 100 people subscribed to it so far.  
We've covered a variety of topics that aren't relevant to this forum here 
and for the most part it's worked out very well.  That is, until 
yesterday. 

Yesterday, a fundamentalist christian came online and started in with his 
condemming of the members of the list (much like you often see in 
alt.homosexuality or alt.politics.homosexuality).  This in turn led to an 
increasing amount of bad feelings on the list and what is best described 
as a smouldering flame war.

I've received several requests from list members asking me do something,
and I concur that this person's presence is at the very least not
conducive to a beneficial mailing list.  Normally, I dont' have a problem
with a little heated discussion, but the purpose of this list IS NOT to
have a flame fest. 

The question I have is, ethically, is there anything wrong with removing 
this person?  I would not doing it because I don't care for what he says, 
but because the forum is not proper for it and there are better places.

In any case, I am enclosing a copy of most of the introduction material 
that is sent to new users.  I removed the portions that talk specifically 
about dealing with the mailing server.

----------------

Hi there.  Welcome aboard.

This mailing list is to serve as a discussion forum for Lesbian, Gay, 
Bisexual and Transgender people living in the midwest.  Unlike many of 
the larger mailing lists, one of my goal is to provide an atmosphere that 
is more personal and friendly in nature.  In time, it is my hope that we 
all get to know each other by name instead of just an impersonal email 
address.

To that end, I'd like to welcome more generic and general discussions.  
Talk to us about whats happening in your town on on your campus.  Got a 
party you are planning?  Let us know.  Is there a conference happening in 
your town?  Clue us in.  

Essentially, I want this to increase communication among the digital 
queers in the part of the country that most people forget is here.

As for what areas this list covers.  It is physically located in Mankato,
Minnesota, which is about an hour south of Minneapolis/St. Paul.  But, it 
is very difficult to determine what exactly the 'midwest' is, so 
basically the policy is 'if you think you live in the midwest, then this 
list is for you.'

The topic of the list is basically as a hang-out forum for queer 
peoples.  Unlike many of the more focused lists, this list is very open 
on the topics and we welcome this.  The only guideline is that flames of 
any kind are not welcome and when people say 'take it to email', please 
be kind enough to do just that.

