Wrong.  Title 42 of the U.S. Code pertains to any person, local government,
or federal government.  The specific sections are Section 1981 (civil action
for interference with the "full and equal benefits of all laws for the
security of persons and property"), Section 1982 (civil action for
interference with equal rights to "inherit, purchase, lease, sell, hold, and 
convey" property), Section 1983 (civil action for anyone, under the color of
state law, deprives the plaintiffs of rights guaranteed by federal law or
the U.S. Constitution), Section 1985 (civil action for conspiracy to
interfere with rights under the U.S. Constitution and federal law), and
Section 1987 (civil action for the failure to prevent the conspiracy in
Section 1985).

Successful recent actions under Section 1983 for violation of First
Amendment include:

Albaum v. Carey
Bart v. Telford
Dennis v. County School Board
Ghadiali v Delaware State Medical Society
Gonzales v Benavides
Hillis v Stephan F, Austin State University
Hughes v Rizzo
Jacobs v Stratton
Johnson v Lincoln University
Lewis v Blackburn
Providence Journal Co v McCoy

Title 42 of the U.S. Code is not recent.  It dates to the Ku Klux Klan Act
of 1871.  The language was intended to provide a powerful tool to protect
and preserve constitutional rights.  The U.S. Supreme Court recognized in
Wade v. Smith that Section 1983 creates a whole species of tort liability.


