Here is my shot at clarifying:

============================
Types of government-owned forums, i.e. places where expressive
activity takes place:

    1. (Traditional) public forum: Open all people and all topics;
       expressive activity can't be prohibited:
             Example: A City-owned park
    2. Limited (or designated, or created) public forum:
       Open to all or some people and all or some topics; can
       be shut down for the right reasons.
             Example: A typical student newspaper at a state university
    3. Nonpublic forum: Open only to those with some special need
       for some special purpose:
             Example: A typical staff newspaper at a state university (?)
=============================

All these terms are clearer if they are prefaced with the phrase
"government-owned". I may start using this terminology:
       
       1. Government-owned public forum
       2. Government-owned limited public forum
       3. Government-owned nonpublic forum

... which I think is less confusing.

