Once again, you grossly over-simplify things by rambling through a dissent
that is several pages and then pulling out a paragraph that does not even
explicitly defend your position.  Moreover, your ellipsis is extremely
misleading, since the two sentences do not appear in the same paragraph at all.
Your second sentence appears nowhere in my copy of Brennan's dissent.  The
paragraph actually ends, "This prohibition is implicit on the Mosley line of
cases, in Tinker, and in those cases in which we have approved content-based
restrictions on access to government property that is not a public forum."

First, the dissent argues that the case revolves around equal access, not
absolute access.  "(The) Court fundamentally misperceives the essence of
(PLEA's claims).  This case does not involve an 'absolute access' claim.
It involves an 'equal access' claim.  As such it does not turn on whether
the internal mail system is a 'public forum'.  In focusing on the public
forum issue, the Court disregards the First Amendment's central 
proscription against censorship, in the form of viewpoint discrimination,
in any forum, public or private."  This is the second sentence in the
opinion

Second, the dissent argues explicitly that the Court's public forum analysis
is irrelevant.  Two paragraphs below the sentence you quote: "Addressing the
question of viewpoint discrimination directly, free of the Court's irrelevant
public forum analysis, it is clear that the exclusive access policy
discriminates on the basis of viewpoint."

Third, they go on to shred the idea that the exclusion of PLEA was reasonable.
They start with, "On a practical level, the only reason for the petitioner to
seek an exclusive access policy is to deny its rivals access to an effective
channel of communication.  No other group is explicitly denied access to the
mail system.  In fact, as the Court points out, many other groups have been
granted access to the system.  Apparently, access is denied to the
respondents because of the likelihood of their expressing points of view
different from the petitioner's on a range of subjects.  The very argument
the petitioner advances in support of the policy, the need to preserve labor
peace, also indicates the access policy is not viewpoint neutral."  They
go on to argue that the policy is simultaneously overinclusive,
underinclusive, and furthers no discernible state interests.  They conclude
with, "Although the state's interest in preserving labor peace in the
schools in order to prevent disruption is unquestionably substantial,
merely articulating the interest is not enough to sustain the exclusive
access policy in this case.  There must be some showing that the articulated
interest is advanced by the policy.  In the absence of such a showing, the
exclusive access policy must fall."


