they are to limited public forums.  The Grayned case I mentioned already
makes that point.  The petitioners were protesting on a sidewalk 100 feet
away from a school building.

You and Carl and others keep implying that reasonable means jolly well
anything a judge or jury thinks is reasonable.  It simply does not.  It
has a very specific meaning, and if you read O'Brien, Grayned, Board of
Airport Commissioners, Los Angeles City Council, Kovacs, etc., you would
find very illustrative examples of precisely what the Court is referring
to.  Of course, that means you have to follow the chain of reasoning of
the Court via its citations and not just ramble around in search of
snippets to run together with ellipses from far-flung parts of the
written opinions.


