As long as a manner restriction does not abridge their right to reach as
many audiences as they like (i.e., they can mega cross-post), I think a
restriction that they post as few identical copies of the message might
pass the reasonableness standard for time, place, manner restrictions.

To pass muster, the restrictions must be specifically tailored to further
a significant government interest, the policy must in fact further that
interest, the policy must abridge free expression in as limited a fashion
consistent with furthering the significant government interest, the
government interest itself must not be suppression of expressive
communication, and the policy must be viewpoint neutral.  This is what
the Supremes mean by reasonable restrictions on time, place, and manner.


