Any discussion of private sites is very murky.  In the absence of a state
statute, state constitutional provision, or state judicial provision to
the contrary, the Supremes would allow private sites wide latitude.
Private universities and commercial access providers might be a different
story.  That would be hard to guess.

Could a poster be sued for reasonably forseeable reactions, my guess is
an almost certain "no" from the Supremes.  The Supremes have been very
clear on punishing a speaker for audience reactions -- forseeable or not.
The term of art for that is a "Heckler's Veto".  If members of an audience
commit a crime, those members of the audience are to be punished and not
the speaker.  The same with tort claims.  If members of the audience damage
property, they pay for the damages and not the speaker.

Could a site put a limit on the number of groups a message can be
cross-posted to?  With private sites, the Supremes will be inclined
to give wide latitude.  With a government site, I think the Supremes
would not feel such a restriction is reasonable under the criteria I
mentioned earlier in this message.


Mitch

