That is a different question.

The heckler's veto concerns making a speaker responsible for the actions of
audience because the audience reacts with hostility as a result of the
expressive communication.  In the case of Skokie, one of the arguments the
city tried to raise was, if the Nazis march in Skokie, the residents may
react violently.  The response of the Court was, "Too bad.  If residents
react violently, then arrest them.  That is no excuse for abridging free
speech."

Yelling "Fire!" in a crowded theatre involves the intent not to express
an idea but to incite an audience to an immediate reaction.  The point is
not to challenge or confront or inform or explain but simply to incite a 
stampede, riot, etc.

When there is no fire and someone yells "Fire!" in a crowded theatre,
the intent of the speaker is then apparent.  In the case of spamming,
there will almost invariably be ambiguity regarding the speaker's intent
(unless the spammer is dumb enough to admit that he wanted to cause systems
to fail).  If there was uncontrovertible evidence that the intention of the
spammer was not to reach a wider audience and instead to disrupt the system,
then I suppose that the systems could bring a cause of action for damages.
Criminal prosecution might even be possible under malicious mischief
statutes, etc.


