In article <2v574u$sfm@mailer.fsu.edu> Mitch <gmitchel@garnet.acns.fsu.edu> writes:
>If you want to restrict people to mega cross-posting in order to spam,
>I think the restriction would be reasonable.  However, if you want to
>claim that mega cross-posting leads to an inode shortage, and therefore
>no mega cross-posting, either, then I think the restriction becomes
>unreasonable.  Now you are restricting the number of audiences a user
>can reach with a single message.

I've been trying to work out where this idea that a x-posted article
uses inodes but not blocks came from.   This is only true if the spool
space is split over multiple filesystems so that a symlink is used instead
of a hard link.   Hard links consume only directory entries and not inodes.
(If the system does not support symlinks then under these circumstances
the blocks would be duplicated on each filesystem as well.)

For this reason and others I feel much more strongly about non-x-posted
spam than a single article x-posted to many groups.

