You're not going to be able to hide it from the superuser if he wants
to get it.  Even if you store it locally, you'll have to upload it to
use it, and a cron job that periodically scanned user directories for 
.newsrcs would be trivial to write.

>     I'll have to see what my permissions *were*.  But I've got another
>question - I've though of downloading the .newsrc (I'm on a DOS to UNIX
>dialup) and just uploading it when I need it.  But it doesn't appear to
>be that simple - what happens to the pointers to "read" and "unread"
>news, and subscribed newsgroups?  There's no sign of 'em if I download
>.newsrc. BTW, I use eep to maintain .newsrc and keep all 4700 newsgroups
>in there, although I only *receive* about 40.

The newsgroup you are subscribed to have a : after the name - those that
you are not subscribed to have a !.  The numbers after the group name
represent the read articles.  So, actually it is that simple.

However, like I said, if the superuser wants to get it, he will get it.
Your best bet if you *really* want to hide it is finding a provider with
an explicit policy of not reading user .newsrcs.

