In article <2v0rgs$p0l@mailer.fsu.edu>
Mitch <gmitchel@garnet.acns.fsu.edu> writes:

> Spamming is expressive communication.  Hence, it is a manifestation
> of the right to free expression.  Free expression is a fundamental
> right in this country.

a)Free expression is a fundemental right, period. Some countries
recognize this, others do not.

b)Spamming is communication to roughly the same extent burping is. 

c)The fundemental right of free expression does not include the right
to use the property of another as a medium.

So, yes -- you have a right, a fundemental right, to post any message
you wish or express any idea you wish. But if no one will permit you
the use of their private tools to do so, you're SOL until you can buy
your own. It's no different than me not having my own late-night talk
show on CBS, my doodles shown in the Guggenheim, or being banned from
spray-painting "Smash the State!" on the White House. The owners of all
of these forms of property do not see my use of them to express my
ideas as being in their best interests. They may or may not be correct,
but that is irrelevant.

"Freedom of the press belongs to he who owns one."
